      "dom_id": "question-70515",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find where the tangent to the curve $y={x}^{5}+2$ at the point $(-1,1)$ intersects the $x$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)={x}^{5}+2.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5}+2)=5{x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(-1)=5(-1)^{4}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =5(x+1) \\\\ y & =5x+5+1 \\\\ y & =5x+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $x$-axis, we set $y=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =5x+6 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(-\\frac{6}{5},0).$",
